# Generated contract artifacts and their source schemas are always LF so the
# contract drift gate (regenerate + `git diff --exit-code`) is byte-stable
# across operating systems and every contributor's core.autocrlf setting. The
# Pydantic exporter and the frontend type generator both emit LF; these
# attributes make the stored blob and every checkout match.
schemas/*.schema.json text eol=lf
frontend/src/contracts/*.schema.json text eol=lf
frontend/src/contracts/*.d.ts text eol=lf

# Human-edited tunables. A config file whose bytes shift with the checkout would
# change the hash the run manifest records for it.
config/*.json text eol=lf

# The committed run state: the eval ledger, the fingerprints, the seen-URL
# store and the feed-health record. Rows are appended by CI on Linux and read by
# a developer on Windows; a CRLF rewrite would show every historical row as
# changed.
#
# `merge=union` because every file here is append-only and every row is
# independent of its neighbours. Two runs that both append are not in
# disagreement, so the union of both sides is the answer and a text merge that
# stops the push costs a reader the whole day. A reader of these ledgers already
# deduplicates: `load_published` and `load_seen` keep the earliest row per
# address, and the eval writer refuses an observation it already holds.
#
# `frontend/public/telemetry/*.csv` is deliberately NOT union. It is a full
# rewrite of `state/item-health/`, so a union of two rewrites is a file with
# every row twice. Assemble regenerates it instead.
state/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union
state/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# The training window. Deliberately NOT `merge=union`: it is a rolling window
# rather than an append-only ledger, so the union of two rolls is a file that
# holds evicted rows again and exceeds `finetune.corpus_rows`. The harvest
# records what its run saw, so a race is answered by replaying those rows onto
# the new base.
corpus/*.jsonl text eol=lf
corpus/*.json text eol=lf
corpus/*.txt text eol=lf

# Test fixtures are byte-exact inputs. A golden summary, a canary payload, or a
# captured page is compared against a fresh pipeline run, so any end-of-line
# conversion would fail the comparison on whichever machine has the other
# core.autocrlf setting.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Source files are edited on Windows and Linux. Keep diffs about code, not line
# endings.
*.py text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.svelte text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Prose is reviewed line by line. A checkout must not rewrite the document.
*.md text eol=lf

# The prompts are fingerprint inputs: `fingerprint.build_inputs` hashes the
# prompt string, so their bytes are part of the determinism stamp. Python's
# `read_text` folds CRLF to LF before that hash, which is why a CRLF copy has
# never broken a fingerprint - but an unpinned file still shows as modified on
# a fresh Linux checkout, and a dirty tree is what aborts the publish push.
*.txt text eol=lf

# The rest of the authored surface. Every one of these was unpinned until a
# CRLF blob in a single doc aborted a rebase and discarded a whole day's
# digest; pinning the file type is cheaper than finding the next one.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.ipynb text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
.editorconfig text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Shell a workflow step runs. A CRLF blob here is not a diff nuisance, it is a
# broken job: the runner's bash reads the carriage return as part of the shebang
# and as part of every command.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Published digest payloads. Their sha256 is recorded in the run manifest and
# re-verified, so the stored bytes and the checked-out bytes must agree.
frontend/public/**/*.json text eol=lf
frontend/public/**/*.md text eol=lf

# The month search index's vector sibling: raw int8 laid end to end, not text.
# An end-of-line conversion here would rewrite bytes inside a vector, and every
# offset after it, and each one would still decode without complaint.
frontend/public/assist/index/*.bin binary

# Downloaded, not authored: never diff or eol-convert these.
*.gguf binary
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.onnx binary
*.wasm binary
